WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.205
In a vision transformer,

00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:06.318
a patch on this cat's eye can look straight
across the image at the other eye.

00:00:06.338 --> 00:00:08.275
How does it decide where to look?

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:13.568
Cut the photo into a grid: fourteen by ten, a
hundred and forty patches.

00:00:13.588 --> 00:00:17.075
Each patch becomes a token, a short list of
numbers.

00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:22.463
Ours has just two, made by hand: how green the
patch is, and how much edge it holds.

00:00:22.482 --> 00:00:25.138
Every patch lands as a dot on this plane.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:27.870
A trained model learns hundreds of its own.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.057
One patch asks a question: its query is this
orange arrow.

00:00:32.077 --> 00:00:35.595
Every patch offers a key, here simply its own
dot.

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:38.557
Compare the query with each key by a dot
product:

00:00:38.577 --> 00:00:42.345
how far the key reaches along the arrow, times
the arrow's length.

00:00:42.365 --> 00:00:45.682
The background points away, so it scores below
zero.

00:00:45.702 --> 00:00:49.140
The other eye reaches furthest: ten point
five.

00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:52.962
Divide every score by root d, here root two.

00:00:52.982 --> 00:00:58.025
Then a softmax turns scores into weights: all
positive, adding up to one.

00:00:58.045 --> 00:01:00.970
The other eye's centre takes sixty three
percent.

00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:05.238
Each patch also carries a value, here simply
its pixels.

00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:08.075
The output is the weighted average of the
values:

00:01:08.095 --> 00:01:11.045
mostly eye, borrowed from across the face.

00:01:11.655 --> 00:01:14.110
A real head has sixty four numbers per key,

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.160
so raw scores come out about eight times
larger.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.872
Skip the division by root d, and what happens
to these weights?

00:01:20.892 --> 00:01:22.605
Pause and decide.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.468
They collapse.

00:01:26.488 --> 00:01:30.742
One patch takes all but a few billionths of
the weight, and the rest vanish.

00:01:30.762 --> 00:01:34.068
Their gradients vanish too, so the head stops
learning.

00:01:34.087 --> 00:01:37.500
Dividing by root d keeps scores near unit
size.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.990
And every patch asks at once.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.865
An ear finds the other ear.

00:01:41.885 --> 00:01:44.565
Blurry background finds blurry patches.

00:01:44.585 --> 00:01:49.265
The pink nose finds the pink ears: our
features match colour, not meaning.

00:01:49.285 --> 00:01:52.410
Stack every row, and you get the attention
matrix.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:58.035
Vision transformers, the D-E-T-R detector and
CLIP repeat this in every layer.

00:01:58.055 --> 00:02:02.848
But every patch scores every patch: nineteen
thousand six hundred pairs.

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:06.555
Halve the patch size, and the work grows
sixteen times.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.062
So remember three things.

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:12.625
A score is the query dot the key, divided by
root d.

00:02:12.645 --> 00:02:15.300
A softmax turns scores into weights.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.245
And the output is the weighted sum of the
values.

